Change(genesis='e30427e699992327267add669c3bc989f6d16525ba6f493121eec7778638e0ce', deps=('c48a69f7571d25b74df64e2c773347266c9f385da8f53c3c41986c51a60ee937',), event=Authored(todo='frameworksplit', at=datetime(2026, 11, 26, 20, 6, 13, 845010), actor='bmabsout', kind='suzateryTodo', created=datetime(2026, 10, 17, 0, 0, 0), body='Split the web framework into its own public repo, pinned by rev: the page algebra (PageState, step, draw, Source, frame, reading, combinators), the shell (build-addressed loader, service worker, new-build offer, revalidation, placeholder) and the e2e harness (CDP driver, scenes, journal, shots)', spec=Flat(value=0.75), rationale=('The framework changes far less often than the pages and has its own laws, so its own repository and cache pay off.',), category='', waiting_on='', detail="What done means, added on 2026-11-21. One sentence for the boundary, at the head of the framework's own README: the view tree is the interactive layer (state, a pure step, a view, patches a painter applies), and Typst is the content inside it (rich text, documents and the design's tokens, compiled in a closed world); no view is a Typst document over state. And a first run with no deployment behind it: one command builds and serves a small local-first app from the framework's repository, with no NixOS, no tailnet and no model. The second app (peerchat) is the measure of how much of an app is still not the framework's; it is worth most after the library holds its indexed backend and the store primitives now written in this application.\n\nTo investigate once the framework stands in its own repository: programs composed as state machines (a program takes messages and gives effects; two programs side by side, one of two, or one nested in another), so that what the desk does by hand to host several pages becomes the framework's own composition. The design note of programkinds names the question and what decides whether it is worth a stage.", source=None, subtodos=(), notes=(), note=''))